  <article class="documentation-content col-md-9">
    <h1>Documentation</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      How this system works. What happens when you search the collections or ask AI a question.
    </p>

    <section id="overview">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The collections come from the Stanford Digital Repository (SDR) and are indexed in
        <a href="https://solr.apache.org/">Apache Solr</a>, a search engine. Each item is stored as a
        metadata record (title, authors, dates, collection, and so on) together with its full text,
        which is split into passages of roughly 1,200 characters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every passage also has an <i>embedding</i>: a list of numbers that represents what the
        passage means. Passages about similar topics have similar embeddings, even when they use
        different words. Embeddings are what make semantic search possible.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="search-modes">
      <h2>How a search mode is chosen</h2>
      <p>
        When you use Search, you don't need to pick a search mode. The app looks at the shape of your query and chooses
        keyword, semantic, or hybrid search for you.
      </p>
      <table class="table table-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">If your query looks like…</th><th scope="col">The app uses…</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A quoted phrase, a field search such as title, an identifier (druid or DOI), or a title-like phrase</td>
            <td>Keyword</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A question (“How did…”, “What is…”, or ending in “?”)</td>
            <td>Semantic, or hybrid if the question includes names or exact terms</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A long, natural-language description</td>
            <td>Semantic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Anything else</td>
            <td>Hybrid</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

    <p class="text-muted">
      The chosen search mode is displayed at the bottom of the search results page after "Search strategy".
    </p>
    </section>

    <section id="keyword-search">
      <h3>Keyword search</h3>
      <p>
        Keyword search matches the words you typed against item metadata and the full-text passages.
        It is the best choice for exact titles, names, and identifiers, where you know the precise
        words that should appear.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="semantic-search">
      <h3>Semantic search</h3>
      <p>
        Semantic search turns your query into an embedding and looks for passages whose embeddings are
        close to it, that is, passages with a similar meaning. It can find relevant material even
        when it shares no words with your query.
      </p>
      <p>
        An item is ranked by its single best-matching passage, and only passages above a similarity
        threshold count, so loosely related material is left out.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="hybrid-search">
      <h3>Hybrid search</h3>
      <p>
        Hybrid search runs a keyword search and a semantic search separately, then merges the two
        ranked lists using a method called <i>Reciprocal Rank Fusion</i>. Items that rank
        highly in either list rise to the top, and items that do well in both rank highest. This
        gives a good balance when a query mixes specific terms with a broader idea.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="matching-text">
      <h2>Matching text</h2>
      <p>
        The snippets shown under each search result are the passages from that item that best matched
        your query, with matching words highlighted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ai-summary">
      <h2>AI summary (“About these results”)</h2>
      <p>
        When a search has results, an AI model writes a short overview of the items on the current
        page. The summary is based only on their metadata, such as titles, authors, and abstracts.
        It does not search any further or read the items’ full text.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="ask-ai">
      <h2 class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
        <%= render "shared/ai_icon", class_name: "ai-prompt-icon" %>
        Ask AI
      </h2>
      <p>Ask AI answers questions about the collections in a conversation. For each question:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Your question is sent along with the conversation so far. Conversations are not saved.</li>
        <li>The AI model searches the collections using read-only search tools: the same keyword, semantic, and hybrid searches described above, plus a search over individual passages.</li>
        <li>It reads the most relevant passages from the items it finds.</li>
        <li>It writes an answer that cites the items it used, with page numbers where available.</li>
        <li>Before the answer is shown, any link that doesn’t point to a source the AI actually retrieved is removed.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        From a search results page, <strong>Ask AI about these results</strong> starts a conversation that
        already knows your current search, including any filters, so you can ask follow-up questions
        about those items.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section id="limitations">
      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Answers come only from these collections, not from the wider web.</li>
        <li>Answers are not exhaustive. The AI reads a sample of the most relevant material, not every matching item.</li>
        <li>The number of searches the AI can run for a single answer is capped.</li>
        <li>AI models can make mistakes. Check the cited sources before relying on an answer.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</div>
